From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivo Nunes <ivo@nunes.uk>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:26:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Physics: add space_shift() to move a whole space at once
 (Jolt)

A floating origin moves a large world's physics bodies with it. Moved
one by one through body_set_state(), each is a separate update, and a
kinematic body works out a velocity from the jump, so a kilometre in one
step flings whatever it touches.

PhysicsServer3D.space_shift() moves everything in a space at once, as a
teleport: nothing is woken or given velocity, constraints and soft
bodies' vertices (held in their bodies' frames) come along, and a
kinematic body's target moves too. Jolt's broad phase is rebuilt once
afterwards (OptimizeBroadPhase()): left, the next step rebuilt it
piecemeal, 20 to 33 ms with a city's thousands of bodies. It returns
false where an engine can't do it (Godot Physics, which keeps the
default), so callers fall back to moving objects one by one.

No upstream issue yet.

Test: with Jolt, static bodies under a world shifted by (-1000, 0, 0)
are hit by rays 1 km away and not where they were, and those outside it
where they stayed (Purr's samples/world). On a city of about 32,000
nodes, the physics step after a shift stays at its usual 2.5 ms.

